# Optional, defaults to "development" (Node's own default when unset). Set this to
# "production" in the real deployment - several things key off it: better-auth's rate limiter
# is only on by default in production (see RATE_LIMIT_* below), and its dev-mode IP fallback
# only kicks in when this is "development".
NODE_ENV=development

# It must be at least 32 characters and generated with high entropy.
# You can also use openssl rand -base64 32 to generate one.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
# The server listens on the port in this URL (see src/index.ts) - when deploying
# behind Docker or a reverse proxy, keep this the same as the URL clients actually use.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:5000

# Comma-separated list of client origins allowed to call this server (CORS) and trusted
# by better-auth for CSRF/redirect checks (see src/lib/cors.ts). Update this when you
# deploy client origins beyond localhost.
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:8000

DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/dbname

# All three below are optional - shown here at their defaults. Raise DB_POOL_MAX as concurrent
# traffic grows (keep it well under Postgres's max_connections, shared across all app instances).
# DB_CONNECTION_TIMEOUT_MS has no equivalent default in node-postgres itself (it defaults to no
# timeout, i.e. a hang) - we default it to 5s here so a dead/unreachable DB fails fast instead.
DB_POOL_MAX=10
DB_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
DB_CONNECTION_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# All three below are optional - shown here at their defaults. They bound the raw HTTP
# connection (separate from the DB timeouts above) so a slow/stalled client can't hold a
# connection open forever. HEADERS_TIMEOUT_MS must be <= REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS, and
# KEEP_ALIVE_TIMEOUT_MS must be < HEADERS_TIMEOUT_MS (validated at startup in src/lib/config.ts).
# Once this sits behind a reverse proxy/load balancer, raise KEEP_ALIVE_TIMEOUT_MS above that
# proxy's own idle timeout, or the proxy can try to reuse a connection this server just closed.
REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
HEADERS_TIMEOUT_MS=20000
KEEP_ALIVE_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# Both optional - shown here at their defaults (also better-auth's own defaults). These set the
# *general* rate limit; better-auth already applies its own tighter built-in limits to
# sign-in/sign-up/password-reset regardless of these values, so no extra config is needed there.
# Whether rate limiting is enabled at all follows NODE_ENV above (on only in production).
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=10
RATE_LIMIT_MAX=100

# Optional, defaults to false. better-auth keys rate limiting by the X-Forwarded-For header,
# trusting a single-value header unconditionally - with no reverse proxy in front of this
# service (the current setup), a direct client could set that header to a new value on every
# request and bypass rate limiting entirely. Leave this false until a real reverse proxy sits
# in front of this service and overwrites that header itself; src/index.ts uses this flag to
# overwrite X-Forwarded-For with the real TCP peer address otherwise. Flip to true only once
# such a proxy exists.
TRUST_PROXY=false